#!/usr/bin/nft -f
#
# Restrict chibi's dream-journal sync port (tcp/8077) to loopback and the
# paired peer.
#
# Dream sync is peer-to-peer: each chibi runs a small HTTP server exposing its
# own dream entries, and each pulls the other's every few minutes. Serving
# means binding 0.0.0.0:8077, which puts the journal on the LAN.
#
# ── THIS IS THE SECOND CONTROL, NOT THE FIRST ────────────────────────────────
# The real one is dream_sync_token, checked as an X-Sync-Token header. Read the
# auth predicate before trusting it:
#
#     return not token or self.headers.get("X-Sync-Token") == token
#
# An EMPTY token authenticates everyone. So a config that never sets one -- the
# committed default is the literal string "change-this-shared-secret", and a
# blank is worse -- serves the journal to anyone who asks. Set a real token on
# both machines; this file only narrows who may ask.
#
# ── AND IT IS BEST-EFFORT, WHICH synapd-bridge.socket IS NOT ─────────────────
# That socket has Requires= on its guard, so a failed guard means the port
# never opens. Nothing equivalent is possible here: chibi is a session
# application that binds the port itself, so if this table fails to load, chibi
# still listens and is simply unfenced. Do not read the presence of this file
# as a guarantee -- check the table is actually loaded:
#
#     sudo nft list table inet chibi_dreamsync_guard
#
# ── SITE-SPECIFIC ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The peer below is this network's Raspberry Pi. Anyone reusing this file MUST
# change it -- an allowlist naming someone else's host is an allowlist of one
# arbitrary machine on yours.
#
# Its own table, so it cannot disturb synguard's `inet synnet`, the
# synapd-bridge guard, or /etc/nftables.conf. Chain policy is accept and every
# drop is qualified by `tcp dport 8077`, so nothing else can be caught by it.
destroy table inet chibi_dreamsync_guard
table inet chibi_dreamsync_guard {
    chain input {
        type filter hook input priority filter; policy accept;
        iif lo accept
        tcp dport 8077 ip saddr 192.168.40.248 counter accept   # chibi @ raspberrypi.lan
        tcp dport 8077 counter drop                             # everyone else; also IPv6,
                                                                # which cannot match ip saddr
    }
}
